    "title": "Cam Young Accused Of Rules Violation During Final Round of The Masters",
    "text": "Did Cam Young get away with a rules violation in the final round of The Masters on Sunday afternoon?The 28-year-old Young is currently at -10 for the tournament. He's two strokes back of the leader, Justin Rose, who's at -12 through 10 holes. Young is in a tie for third place, with Tyrrell Hatton. Rory McIlroy is all alone in second place.AdvertisementBut viewers noticed that Young might have gotten away with a rules violation while in the trees on the ninth hole. Young's caddie appeared to help his golfer align before the shot. However, fans believe that Young never reset after the caddie helped him.Cameron Young on the PGA Tour. (Photo by James Gilbert/Getty Images)James Gilbert/Getty ImagesMultiple viewers of The Masters pointed it out on social media.\"Cam Young punch out on hole #9. He never reset with caddie helping align. If a caddie aligns a player and the player takes a stance without resetting, it is a two-stroke penalty in stroke play under USGA Rule 10.2b(4),\" one fan wrote.Advertisement\"When Cam Young hit his second shot in number 9 from out of the trees, he addressed the ball and then his caddy stood behind and lined him up. Young did not re-address the ball. Violation of rule 10.2b,\" one fan added.\"Your caddie cannot stand behind you (on or close to an extension of the line of play behind the ball) while you are over the ball taking your stance for the stroke, even just to verbally confirm your direction. This is a breach of Rule 10.2b(4) in the Rules of Golf,\" one fan added.\"I thought the same thing and came to twitter to see if others saw it,\" one fan added.AdvertisementCam Young never re-grouped/stepped off/re-aligned in any way after his caddie appeared to help with alignment here on 9.Penalty? No penalty? pic.twitter.com/mMIzkBYRx0— Mark Harris (@itismarkharris) April 12, 2026The back nine will be very interestingIt'll be interesting to see if the CBS broadcast brings this up with Young's back nine on Sunday. It's possible a Masters official, or someone else at the tournament, also noticed a possible rules violation.Young is currently at -10 through nine holes, only two strokes back of the leaders, but it could get a lot worse if he was hit with a two-stroke penalty for the alleged rules violation.The final round of The Masters is airing on CBS.AdvertisementThis story was originally published by The Spun on Apr 12, 2026, where it first appeared in the Golf section. Add The Spun as a Preferred Source by clicking here.",
